ferential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-4$ and  $y=-1$ are equilibrium points\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the phase line of the differential equation?" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318191 1113 #uuid "f792436d-ecaa-5cd1-8320-41fd8629919e" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318191 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each condition:\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ and $y=-1$ are equilibrium points. We represent this on the phase line by drawing two distinct points at these locations.\n\n- We're given that $y=-1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-1$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\nTherefore, our phase line is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/8a5f3a5965878cba364ee0e96af77bea41d53e6b2bd440340d72215e30db3a83.png)" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318193 13194139534897 true] [17592186318192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318194 13194139534897 true] [17592186318192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318195 13194139534897 true] [17592186318192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318196 13194139534897 true] [17592186318192 1000