2f8ab485495b501fea366fa15a8ab.png" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534897 true] [17592186318197 1111 #uuid "f0a7c0d1-cb7f-5389-bb50-173c9b5a242a" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318198 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318199 13194139534897 true] [17592186318198 1089 "q-98715" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318198 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534897 true] [17592186318198 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318198 1113 #uuid "93b52619-a3ee-55f5-8942-7104450a0db2" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318198 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/df340c40982e6d22ef8a815bf043f84fd78b43b4ddc1763845d03d4b2c793aee.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318200 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318201 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318202 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318203 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318204 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1041 "selection" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1104 #edb/ref 17592186318202 13194139534897 true] [17592186318199 1110 #uuid "9c99b2f7-05c8-5c7e-b56c-4c32b3445515" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318200 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318200 1111 #uuid "70100f06-56dd-5f91-9ea1-c05163b336ad" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318201 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318201 1111 #uuid "4d67a8ab-7c36-581f-be90-fefbb9eb5c88" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318202 1034 "III only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318202 1111 #uuid "15f527b7-3019-5235-b977-583168af8f5c" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318203 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318203 1111 #uuid "e751a523-e83d-5f62-a55c-7d20e69f7d6d" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318204 1034 "II only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318204 1111 #uuid "7fd7fdcc-bc9b-5382-81eb-7cf4a201eea4" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318205 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318206 13194139534897 true] [17592186318205 1089 "q-98752" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318205 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534897 true] [17592186318205 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-1$ is a source." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318205 1113 #uuid "6ece7eaa-a6b2-5c98-ac8f-17a221444654" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318205 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318207 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318208 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318209 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318210 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318211 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1041 "selection" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1104 #edb/ref 17592186318209 13194139534897 true] [17592186318206 1110 #uuid "04cce166-b33d-540b-92e7-c67589729173" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318207 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318207 1111 #uuid "6b70ad7f-1fe9-5b11-a0d5-ace26bf2aa94" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318208 1034 "I only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318208 1111 #uuid "78e38bb7-38ae-5c70-8810-997bf249da59" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318209 1034 "III only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318209 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318209 1111 #uuid "a5b6c43b-d176-5602-9953-4c6b81f061d0" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318210 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318210 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318210 1111 #uuid "2836ffe3-3d50-5f28-854e-f1df42512859" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318211 1034 "II only" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318211 1111 #uuid "8908aead-f581-59e9-a80d-2d52eb89778a" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318212 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318213 13194139534897 true] [17592186318212 1089 "q-98757" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318212 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534897 true] [17592186318212 1100 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318212 1113 #uuid "43ca8274-86e6-5327-bd53-afa9903c842d" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318212 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/df68345e3424f1e0020a98ef8dd13456865412818067a822c01c3f50e49386ec.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318214 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318215 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318216 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318217 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318218 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1041 "selection" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1104 #edb/ref 17592186318217 13194139534897 true] [17592186318213 1110 #uuid "a6947406-3cc5-5a93-be13-f64945ef97c9" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318214 1034 "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318214 1111 #uuid "b2712c34-0ab6-5a3a-8a93-2c9c9304db7f" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318215 1034 "$y=0$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318215 1111 #uuid "94606cbc-892f-5dc1-a035-15608e3b3bc1" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318216 1034 "$y=0$ and $y=2$ are both nodes" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318216 1111 #uuid "72a42852-2a96-5aae-933f-a20dc4a55746" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318217 1034 "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318217 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318217 1111 #uuid "2ec5ac98-4242-53b5-b436-9ea845cc2edf" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318218 1034 "$y=0$ and $y=2$ are both sources" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318218 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318218 1111 #uuid "089d3233-051f-5051-a3d6-78c03d1fabbc" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318219 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318220 13194139534897 true] [17592186318219 1089 "q-98758" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318219 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534897 true] [17592186318219 1100 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-6?$" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318219 1113 #uuid "39f28e41-f7f6-5210-a3c5-5a290e07fa3b" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318219 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-6 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3d2eec912d188cd569c4f87558a148c808ee2c815eeca7289103f11465d98859.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-2$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318221 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318222 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318223 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318224 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318225 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1041 "selection" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1104 #edb/ref 17592186318224 13194139534897 true] [17592186318220 1110 #uuid "b7a88794-7b68-54e4-bcae-0c2c83c98251" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318221 1034 "$y=-2$ and $y=3$ are both nodes" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318221 1111 #uuid "cc7003af-528d-5bd0-a31f-dd5aa6ef00d2" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318222 1034 "$y=-2$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318222 1111 #uuid "8b1ab26b-44fd-5d0a-a2e1-0accb2559e05" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318223 1034 "$y=-2$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318223 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318223 1111 #uuid "8dcde911-bb10-51ba-b5a6-5edfcab188bd" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318224 1034 "$y=-2$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318224 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318224 1111 #uuid "4ed28cf1-eb22-5040-843c-4936d6603f77" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318225 1034 "$y=-2$ and $y=3$ are both sinks" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318225 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318225 1111 #uuid "c9971149-1f06-53c6-a99c-ffc724d0f6a6" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318226 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318227 13194139534897 true] [17592186318226 1089 "q-98760" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318226 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534897 true] [17592186318226 1100 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=-(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9)?$" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318226 1113 #uuid "e1da3364-5c3f-500b-bc7c-7a5f92c432cb" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318226 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\"." 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318228 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318229 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318230 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318231 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318232 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1041 "selection" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1104 #edb/ref 17592186318229 13194139534897 true] [17592186318227 1110 #uuid "b4ec143c-b984-58eb-93ce-e72cae8bb019" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318228 1034 "$y=-3$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534897 true] [17592186318228 1111 #uuid "55758d1e-d33b-5677-8be9-3f33f97c7154" 13194139534897 true] [17592186318229 1034 "$y=-3$ is a n